  "opposed": {
    "grade": "cross-model-fresh-thread",
    "at": "2026-08-30T09:05:00Z",
    "raised": 17,
    "model": "gpt-5.6-sol",
    "thread": "codex subagent, spine v2",
    "note": "Two cross-model passes. The first attacked the spine (6 findings); the second attacked the finished prose (9), aimed at the two sections never challenged. Six were correct and changed the text: the 50.6% figure now claims dependence on which reviewers were drawn rather than noise; choice blindness is narrowed to fluency not being evidence; MASAI is separated from the autonomy claim; the forecast target is narrowed to named regulators and published guidance; the uneven-capability claim is marked as expectation, not measurement; and mechanism transfer is labelled as inference. Three are open with the author: the financial-statements analogy, the discovery-exposure risk of detailed disclosure, and the self-implicating line. A third pass attacked the reasoning section alone and found five: Soon et al. covers a trivial task at near-chance prediction and cannot carry deliberated judgement; 'constructed after' does not entail 'not the cause'; the human/model symmetry equivocates because a model has no introspective access by construction; and the strong version proves too much, since it would empty the article's own case for disclosure. All four are answered by moving the claim from 'the account is false' to 'the account cannot be checked', which is also what connects it to the recommendation: a check leaves a trace, a reason does not. The author reviewed the third pass and reaffirmed the strong human/model parallel as a position to stand behind; the published text states the parallel as exact, and the boundary record on c31/c33 carries where the reconstruction-can-be-accurate objection still applies."
  },
